// CARD a4-01 · SEE: the real 2004 annual report flicks past page by page and stops on the financial highlights; on "out" the camera reads into one forgotten line: profit before special items, 2004 · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@page_turn;pages:lego_doc_annualreport_2004_cover,lego_doc_annualreport_2004_new_direction,lego_doc_annualreport_2004_income_statement,lego_doc_annualreport_2004_financials;stop:part;marks:.47,.163,.506,.18@out;zoom:out;z:2.4;lab:2004;in:right"
Here's the part the usual story leaves **out**.

// CARD a4-02 · SEE: a boy on a living-room floor surrounded by studded plastic bricks, building · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_boy_studded_bricks_floor;move:in;z:.18;out:right"
In that worst year, the toy business itself, before write-downs, made a **profit**.

// CARD a4-03 · SEE: the highlights table on the desk; on "hundred" the figure 103 is cut out of the 2004 column and lifts toward us in gold: PROFIT BEFORE SPECIAL ITEMS · DKK 103M · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@doc_lift;doc:lego_doc_annualreport_2004_financials;crop:.1,.1,.8,.3;cut:.47,.163,.506,.18;go:hundred;lab:BEFORE WRITE-DOWNS · DKK 103 MILLION;col:#f2c14e;in:right"
One hundred and three million **kroner**.

// CARD a4-04 · SEE: the real 2004 income statement dim behind; the 2004 net loss as an empty dashed bar; the parts fill it on their words: write-downs DKK 723M on "cleaning", restructuring DKK 502M, closed activities DKK 458M on "closing" · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@headline_split;bg:lego_doc_annualreport_2004_income_statement;head:DKK 1,931M^THE NET LOSS · 2004;total:1931;parts:DKK 723M^WRITE-DOWNS^cleaning~DKK 502M^RESTRUCTURING^restructuring~DKK 458M^CLOSED ACTIVITIES^closing;out:right"
The giant loss came from cleaning up: write-downs, restructuring, and closing what didn't **work**.

// CARD a4-05 · SEE: a big yellow and red LEGO Technic helicopter in a display case, its rotor spinning · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_build_technic;move:in;z:.2;in:right"
But the bricks weren't the problem. LEGO just couldn't see which sets made **money**.

// CARD a4-06 · SEE: a warehouse aisle crammed with boxes on every shelf, the camera pushing down it · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_warehouse_packed_aisle;move:in;z:.3;out:right"
It had no way to cost a single set, so nobody knew which ones lost **it**.

// CARD a4-07 · SEE: parcels travelling one by one along a sorting conveyor past the camera · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_conveyor_parcels;move:right;z:.2;in:right"
Then designers finally checked the boxes, one by **one**.

// CARD a4-08 · SEE: a part-built LEGO Technic car with its motor exposed behind; a beam scale: THE PARTS INSIDE loads on "parts" and the beam slams down on that side; THE BOX'S PRICE on "box" stays high · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@balance_scale;bg:lego_technic_supra_build;l:MORE^THE PARTS INSIDE;r:LESS^THE BOX'S PRICE;lv:3;rv:1;lat:parts;rat:box;out:right"
The worst were sets with a tiny motor or fibre-optic lights: their parts cost more than the whole box sold **for**.

// CARD a4-09 · SEE: three colleagues smiling and nodding at a table, all agreement; the picture slows on "noticed" · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_meeting_agreement;move:in;z:.15;slow:noticed;in:right"
{wry} Every one of those sets lost money, and no one had **noticed**. (J)

// CARD a4-10 · SEE: the real cover of Brick by Brick (David C. Robertson with Bill Breen, 2013) drops onto a dark desk with weight; the book's pages build up under it · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@page_stack;doc:lego_doc_book_brickbybrick_crop;crop:0,0,1,1;land:study;grow:maths;pages:30;lab:BRICK BY BRICK;sub:David C. Robertson · 2013;out:right"
One study later did the maths for nineteen ninety-three to two thousand and **two**.

// CARD a4-11 · SEE: LEGO's dark history gallery behind; profit bars rise year by year 1993–2002 above a zero line (1998 dips red); then on "destroyed" a blue VALUE line sinks under them to −$1.6B · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@profit_vs_value;bg:lego_history_gallery;years:1993,2002;loss:1998;go:reported;vgo:destroyed;val:−$1.6B;vlab:ECONOMIC VALUE;lab:ACCOUNTING PROFIT, EACH YEAR;in:right"
LEGO reported profits in almost every one of those years, yet destroyed about one point six billion dollars of **value**.

// CARD a4-12 · SEE: flames eating through sheets of paper in close-up · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@reel;clip:stock_money_burning_08;move:in;z:.15;out:right"
Profits on paper. Value going **down**.

// CARD a4-13 · SEE: a warehouse of pallets dim behind; one new brick drops in; on each named cost a heavy tag clamps onto it with a chain and it sinks lower: MOULD, COLOUR, SUPPLIER, SHELF SPACE · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@tax_chain;bg:lego_clip_warehouse_stocked;chips:MOULD@mould~COLOUR@colour~SUPPLIER@supplier~SHELF SPACE@shelf;go:piece;lab:THE COMPLEXITY TAX;in:right"
That's the complexity tax: every new piece charges the whole system, the mould, the colour, the supplier, the shelf **space**.

// CARD a4-14 · MOTIF · SEE: an empty toy shop at closing time (AI moment art), half-bare shelves; the brick drops onto the counter; GROWTH THAT MAKES YOU POORER sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_art_toy_store_closed;go:growth;x:.5;y:.66;h:.11;lab:GROWTH THAT MAKES YOU POORER;lx:.5;z:.3;out:right"
When the tax is bigger than what a piece earns, growth makes you **poorer**.
